## 4. Selection and sizing

- Measured insulation diameter and screen cut-back length, both of which are drawing dimensions.
- Pollution level at the site where an outdoor termination is used.
- Whether the cable is single-core or three-core, and how the cores are trained in the joint bay.
- Earthing arrangement, which decides whether a separate screen earth is needed.
- The IEC cable standard version the project is being built to, since geometry can differ between editions.

## 5. Standards commonly specified

- IEC 60502-4, test requirements for accessories for cables from 6 kV up to 30 kV.
- IEC 61442, test methods for accessories above 3.6 kV up to 36 kV.
- IEC 60664-1, insulation coordination and creepage.
- IEC 60840, the cable standard relevant where the same accessory family is applied near the 30 kV boundary.

Standards are listed as those normally cited for this component class. They describe the requirement, not a claim of certification. Test evidence applicable to a specific order is confirmed per purchase order.

## 6. Handling and installation

- Record the screen cut-back length before the sleeve is applied, since it cannot be re-checked after recovery.
- Keep a clean work zone. Grit under the sleeve is a stress point, not a cosmetic defect.
- Respect the cable minimum bend radius while training the cores, particularly in a cramped joint bay.
- Complete the earthing connection before closing the bay.

## 7. Failure modes and mitigation

| Failure mode | Likely cause | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Stress cone not sitting on the screen end | Screen cut-back length taken from a different cable standard or measured loosely | Cut to the drawing for the actual cable and record the measured value before recovery. |
| Outdoor termination flashing over | Creepage below the site pollution requirement, or sheds contaminated at commissioning | Select the shed profile for the pollution class, clean before energisation. |
| Joint leaking after a wet installation | Sheath sealing applied to a damp surface | Dry the sheath, apply sealing parts to drawing, avoid working in standing water. |
| Kit short of parts on site | Parts consumed or lost because they were supplied loose | Use a kit with a fixed bill of materials and check the contents before the outage. |

## 8. Typical applications

- European distribution networks and industrial substations built to IEC practice.
- Solar and wind collection systems using 20 kV cable.
- Ring main units and compact switchgear where an indoor termination is required.
- Infrastructure projects where installation speed matters more than site heating capacity.

